    "after_restore_canonical": "",
    "description": "A restore clears the flags its delete set, and no others (state.md#deletion-yields-to-concurrent-work). A files space signed as the example above is, whose genesis (the first of `entries`) has no ops, and seven entries after it: e1 makes the folder Trip, e2 the files a, b and c in it, e3 deletes c, e4 deletes Trip, and e5 restores Trip, each after the one before. e4 flags Trip, a and b, and not c, which e3 had flagged, so after e5 Trip, a and b are back and c is still deleted: `after_restore` gives each node's flags and `after_restore_canonical` the canonical state's encoding. Then e6 restores c and e7 deletes Trip, both after e5 and so concurrent: the restore names c, which touches c and everything above it, so the delete yields there; `after_both` has Trip and c not deleted, Trip with a delete conflict, and a and b deleted, `after_both_root` the canonical state's BLAKE3.",
